---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const org = "https://github.com/ternmesh";
---

<Base
    title="Privacy"
    description="What the Tern apps for Android and for iPhone, iPad and Mac, and this site, learn about you: nothing they keep."
>
    <section class="wrap" style="padding-top: 4rem; max-width: 44rem">
        <h1>Privacy</h1>
        <p class="muted">Last changed 9 October 2026.</p>
        <p>
            This covers Tern, the app for Android; Tern Mesh, the app for iPhone, iPad and Mac; and this site.
            The project collects nothing about the people who use them: there is no account, no analytics and no
            advertising. What does leave your device for the internet is a request to this site, when you visit
            it or when an app looks for firmware, and the map's tiles, when you open the map. Cloudflare, which
            serves the site, sees the first, as any host does; who serves the map is set out below.
        </p>

        <h2>The apps</h2>
        <p>Both apps work the same way, except where this says otherwise.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Your messages, contacts and groups</strong> are held by your node and kept on
                your device, so that the app can show them. They go between nodes by radio. They
                do not pass through, and are not stored on, any server of the project's.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Bluetooth</strong> is how the app talks to your node, and is used for nothing
                else.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Your location</strong> is used only if you share your position with a contact or
                a group, and the app asks for it only then. While your node shares with anyone, the app
                gives it the phone's location, and the node rounds it to what each one was chosen to see
                before anything goes on the air. It is given to nothing else. On Android 11 and earlier,
                Android also asks for location before an app may look for Bluetooth devices; the app
                uses that permission to find your node.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The camera</strong>, on a phone or iPad, reads the QR code of a contact you
                are adding. The app keeps no picture and sends none anywhere.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Firmware</strong>: when you look for an update to your node's firmware, the app
                fetches the list of releases, and then the firmware, from this site. Those requests
                carry what any request to a website does: the address they come from and the file
                asked for, which for the firmware is the one for your node's board and region. They
                carry nothing else about you or your node.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The map</strong> fetches its tiles while you look at it. The Android app's come
                from <a href="https://openfreemap.org">OpenFreeMap</a>, which sees which areas the map
                shows and nothing else of yours. The Apple app's map is Apple's own, through MapKit,
                which Apple handles under <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/">its privacy
                policy</a>.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            The apps use the internet for nothing else. Removing an app removes everything it kept on
            the device. The Android app is left out of Android's backups and of moving to a new phone:
            the next time it connects, your node gives it back what it holds.
        </p>

        <h2>This site</h2>
        <p>
            The site is static files, with no cookies and no analytics of its own. What the
            <a href="/app">web app</a> remembers, it keeps in your browser. The site is served by
            Cloudflare, which, like any host, sees the address a request comes from and what was asked
            for, and handles that under
            <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">its own privacy policy</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes and questions</h2>
        <p>
            If this changes, the new text and its date will be here, and the change will be in the
            <a href={`${org}/site`}>site's history</a>. Questions go to the issue tracker of the
            <a href={`${org}/android/issues`}>Android app</a>, the
            <a href={`${org}/apple/issues`}>Apple app</a> or the <a href={`${org}/site/issues`}>site</a>.
        </p>
    </section>
</Base>
